hi there Steve Kaufman here today I want  to talk about what to do when you get  frustrated in your language learning  it's a subject that comes up all the  time before I get to that though I just  want to tell you that I have a very  pleasant duty tonight I'm judging a I  guess it's a beauty contest it's Miss  Chinese Vancouver ten Chinese Canadian  girls from Vancouver who are competing  for this Miss Chinese Vancouver title  and the winner will go on to a Miss  Chinese international competition in  Hong Kong and so last week I was one of  four judges that got to interview these  girls and have dinner with them and  today is a big gala event where they  have to you know show off different  dresses traditional Chinese dress or  whatever I think it's a swimsuit thing  here as well and I've got to kind of  judge them out of ten for their  personality their how whose most  photogenic who's you know answers  questions well and so forth and so on  and we got a nice dinner out of it so  yeah I wouldn't have that opportunity if  I didn't speak Cantonese and Mandarin so  there's a another you know advantage to  learning languages but um recently you  know I guess very often I get the  question you know people say well you  know I've I've run out of motivation I'm  not motivated to learn anymore what  should I do this comes up on our forum  at link from time to time there was also  question on our forum someone who is a  student I think in Poland was saying  that her teacher or his teacher her  teacher had asked for her to they she  had to do a test where she has to  identify whether words are formal  informal colloquial um you know academic  or whatever words so I looked through  these two things together and I thought  to myself you know my that my I think  when I did this the video is the seven  secrets to successful language learning  either number one or number  two important secrets were do the things  that you like to do I think the second  one is to spend the time okay so those  are the two big things because it comes  back to the basic idea that language  learning success depends on your  attitude largely and on the amount of  time you put it those are the two big  factors so if you do things that you  like doing that you enjoy doing you're  likely to continue and if you enjoy  doing them you aren't worried about  whether you're meeting some arbitrary  goal I have to be fluent in six months  three months ten months it doesn't  matter you enjoy doing what you're doing  so if I take the example of my check for  example I have had two conversations now  in check I started the check in early  August I've had two conversations with  two of our native speakers at Linc and I  did very poorly I mean struggling to  find words either I didn't have the  words or in many cases I knew the words  but I couldn't recall them um but I  don't find that in any way discouraging  I don't find that in any way  discouraging because what I do with my  check I thoroughly enjoy and I am  reading a book now which is called I've  been at it for a while Sydnee of ropa  vieja I've mentioned it before and I've  been at it for a while you know you  probably can't see this but when I read  it there's all kinds of words in there  that I underlined which I don't know and  then I in many cases pumped them into  link if I'm if I'm up to it or I don't  but I have learned so much about the  history of basically Eastern Central  Europe Poland Hungary and Czechoslovakia  and I'm gathering more and more steam  and my Czech learning I enjoyed it's a  pleasure to go there I also go to my  iPad and there I've got again I don't  know if it's going to be visible or not  but here I've got you know the good  soldier shake and you can see that  there's lots of words that I don't know  and it  in Eiling so if I click on a word it  shows you the meaning at the bottom  you'll see there's a sound bar I can  listen to it  I even have these things in a playlist  so that I just go to it and it's fun  I don't worry about how well I'm doing  and when I don't feel like doing it I  don't do it but those are things that I  enjoy doing with my Russian now I'm at  the stage where I just listen in to echo  my speed because I want to hear the  commentary about you know the recent  elections and things like that but I  don't listen to as much as I used to but  when I do listen I know that all of my  exposure to the language is improving me  so I'm not like that student in Poland  who has to pass a test where you know I  have to identify which words are of this  category or that category formal or  informal or colloquial I can't imagine  that doing that because first of all we  know that the vocabulary in most  contexts is is is 80% 70 to 80% the most  common words of the language so it  doesn't matter what the context is in  formal formal academic business it's 80%  same words so that doing the reading  those exposing yourself to any content  is going to be beneficial I also feel  that to identify a word as informal  formal academic it's very difficult  because if I say in everything all the  words that I'm using in this  presentation this discussion which is a  fairly casual discussion but on a  serious subject all those words can be  used in an informal situation can be  used in an academic paper  I think this poly student said that her  teacher told her his or her told her  that she was not to use phrasal verbs in  academic writing and she was not to use  long phrases like on the one hand on the  other hand in academic writing well to  me writing is writing languages language  and the objective in writing is to be  clear simple and straight  forward unless you're writing a poem or  you're describing beautiful scenery but  most of the articles that we write and  most of our communication we want to be  simple and clear and straightforward and  that's true whether we're speaking or  whether we're writing so I don't see  that all these big differences are all  that meaningful and if I want to get  better at say academic writing then I  read more academic articles but that'll  only work if I'm interested in the  subject very difficult to do if you are  going to try to become better at writing  on veterinary science and you're not  interested in veterinary science you'll  have a real tough time but if you're  very interested in that subject or  chemistry or computers or anything else  and you're reading that stuff all the  time you will naturally acquire the  language the terminology the style that  you need so you know if you stay with  doing things that you enjoy doing and  follow your interests and your interest  can move and you'll go through periods  where you're not so interested and if  you don't always have to look over your  shoulder and say to you know I need this  for my test or am i progressing fast  enough or right you know don't worry  about it like I I owe and that reminds  me you know I was with this other person  a Chinese person who has lived in Canada  for over 10 years and doesn't speak  English very well and and as I was  trying to persuade them they should  really make an effort and they said yeah  yeah yeah but you know I always forget I  was free of course you forget everybody  forgets I mean the number of times you  know in my in my Czech history book here  that I underlined the very same word the  word that I looked up and I forgotten  and that's of course why you make we  highlight them in yellow because you  forget you forget you forget it's all  about forgetting and but with enough  exposure with enough exposure slowly the  brain will learn so if you stay with  things that you enjoy doing whether it  be reading or listening or going out and  talking to people but the bulk of that  is still going to be listening if you  don't speak very well if you're in  conversation with someone you don't have  much to say they have a lot to say and  getting it straight from a native  speaker you know  interactive situation is very very  positive speaking as well I mean I tend  not to do it at first because I don't  have so many words but I know very well  that even those two sessions with a  native speaker on Lync I saw in gaps I  saw words that I knew I that I that I  have and I wasn't able to find them and  and so being confronted with these gaps  in my skills it helps me notice things  it helps me notice and the next time I  came across those words in my reading I  made a particular point of noticing them  so all these different activities are  helpful and if you enjoy reading grammar  by all means read it but if you don't  enjoy reading grammar you don't need to  or you can limit it as I do to the  occasional flip through to see what you  might might glean without trying to nail  things down so I just want to say that  on this issue of you know what happens  when you get frustrated well why are you  frustrated don't worry about how fast  you're progressing be confident that if  you do things that you enjoy doing in  the language and if you stay with it you  will progress you will progress faster  if you do things that you enjoy doing  then if you try to force yourself to do  things that you don't enjoy doing  I have no scientific research to back  that up but I think that's a pretty  common-sense conclusion that I've drawn  from watching people because the vast  majority people who try to force  themselves to do things that they don't  enjoy don't end up progressing very  quickly in the language and those people  that do enjoy what they're doing also  make progress so there you have it how  do you avoid getting frustrated don't  even think about getting frustrated just  do the things that you enjoy doing in  the language thanks for listening bye  for now
 

